WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:02.149 align:start position:0%
 
it is the time of year when a lot of  people spend an unusual amount of time  thinking about death  in late october to early november there  are a number of holidays like the famous  halloween a day of ghosts ghouls and  trick-or-treating and jack-o-lanterns  but there's also the  very close to connected  days of all saints day and its companion  all souls day these three all fall at  around the same time and the  relationship and difference between them  can be a little confusing to a lot of  people i think most of you know what  halloween is and if you don't i've made  an episode previously about that which  you can check out but how about those  other ones all saints day and or souls  day what are these holidays where do  they come from and how are they  celebrated today  [Applause]  all saints day and all souls day are two  interconnected holidays that take place  in late fall specifically in early  november  they are at their core a christian  tradition all saints day is a day of  commemoration of the christian saints  and martyrs a practice that has a long  and interesting history and which is to  complicate things further very strongly  connected to the holiday of halloween  as such we must begin our exploration of  this topic in the ancient celtic pagan  festival known as sarwin  celebrated in pre-christian times and  beginning on october 31st this was a  festival that marked the end of summer  and the beginning of winter  and at this point of transition in the  calendar it was thought that the veil  between the world of the living and that  of the dead was at its thinnest this  meant that one could more easily  communicate with the dead and vice versa  and the festival seems to have very much  revolved around this idea we don't know  all that much with certainty about pagan  practices such as this since most of our  earlier sources come from second-hand  christian writers in the middle ages but  we do know that certain practices like  the lighting of bonfires took place as  well as the general orientation and  theme of this festival  on the other hand as a little religious  movement called christianity developed  in its earliest period they started also  to have feast days commemorating and  celebrating the saints and martyrs of  the faith this is thus a tradition that  goes back to the very earliest days of  christianity  and when this little religion which at  this point had become the state religion  of the largest empire in the world  started to spread to new lands through  missionaries there were different  techniques used to convert the  population in these new lands  and one of the most common and  successful ways to do this has always  been to meet people halfway  what i mean by this is that one would  often take an already existing tradition  or custom and give it new christian  meanings or christian clothing  to christianize customs already present  in the local community  this of course makes it appear like  conversion doesn't make one hat to give  up all of one's entire life and culture  and makes that process a lot more simple  and appealing one often quoted example  of this is that christmas the  celebration of jesus's birth was placed  on december 25th in the roman world  which was the same day that there was  already an existing festival that  celebrated the birth of the gods soul  invictus in the roman empire this theory  is widely rejected by scholars today  however as the evidence doesn't really  point in that direction anymore but  another example one that seems a lot  more likely is that when the christians  came to the british isles the festival  of celebrating the dead saints started  to be celebrated specifically on  november 1st at the same time that the  local custom of sauron was already  taking place  the connection to the world of the  deceased and both of these traditions is  a likely reason that the christians  chose to place the holiday at this  specific time in another attempt to  appropriate local practices and  incorporate them into christianity  rather than eradicate them completely  thus all saints day as we know it was  born and eventually spread to other  places in europe and western christendom  in general  another name used for the holiday was  all hallows day which means that the day  before on october 31st was all hallows  eve which is where we get the name  halloween  and on the day after all saints day on  november 2nd is celebrated all souls day  a festival where one commemorates the  souls of those departed in a general  sense not just the saints as such but  every  soul every faithful soul or any any  person who has died or passed away is  commemorated on all souls day on  november 2nd  this triad of days all hallows eve or  halloween all hallows or saints day and  all souls day is sometimes together  referred to as a hallow tide so as you  might imagine the connection and  difference between these three  somewhat separated days can appear  complicated and confusing to some people  and while it is true that the festival  of halloween originally had its origins  in the  pagan festival of sarwin aside from its  place in the calendar so taking place at  the same time of year there is  very little left of sarwin in what we  know as halloween in fact most of the  things that we associate with halloween  different rituals and imagery and so on  actually comes from later christian  periods and the celebration of all  saints day so for example  trick-or-treating likely originates in a  practice called souling where groups of  people would travel between houses and  singing  asking for  blessings or asking for  prayers for the dead as well as for  objects like asking for apples or soul  cakes as it was known and the famous  jack-o'-lantern which is kind of the  poster  figure for halloween also has its  origins in these practices as people  would often carry with them hollowed out  turnip lanterns as they were going from  house to house so many of these  traditions actually come from a  post-christian period rather than from  the pre-christian sovereign in any case  halloween and all saints day slash all  souls day are usually pretty clearly  distinguished today halloween is a pop  culture infused celebration sometimes  associated with consumerism while all  saints and souls day is a solemn  christian day of commemoration and these  days are widely celebrated across europe  even today however they can look pretty  different depending on where for example  all souls day is also often known as the  day of the dead and in mexico this has  evolved into a very vibrant and unique  tradition of its own strongly flavored  by local culture as seen for example in  the popular pixar film coco  but in a place like sweden which is  where i'm from the celebration of these  holidays looks quite different and it  can also tell us a lot about  how swedes relate to religion and  secularism in sweden both halloween and  all saints day are widely celebrated by  people but they are very clearly  distinguished from each other most of  the time  halloween takes place on october 31st  like  it always does in most places but all  saints day does not actually take place  on the traditional date of november 1st  instead since the 1950s all saints day  actually takes place on the first  saturday in november this means that  every few years the dating of these  holidays do line up in the traditional  way so if halloween falls on a  friday on october 31st that means that  all saints day will fall the next day on  saturday november 1st but if halloween  falls on a sunday like this year that  means that all saints day is celebrated  almost a full week later on november 7th  on this saturday it's weird but that's  how we do it over here and in sweden the  connected holiday of all souls day  supposed to take place on november 2nd  kind of isn't really a thing  the church of sweden does recognize it  as an official day of commemoration but  for the regular swede all saints day and  old souls day are kind of mashed  together in fact what we call all saints  day in sweden  is actually more akin to old souls day  in practice  now sweden is quite a secularized  country it is often ranked among the  most secularized in the world and this  is definitely true to some degree  relatively few swedes identify with a  particular religious denomination and  are often only very mildly engaged in  religious questions but as scholarship  has shown like that by scholar of  religions david turpiel this does not  mean that people don't believe in a  divine higher power for example or that  they have abandoned religious traditions  not at all in fact  christian holidays like all saints day  christmas or easter are still very  actively and passionately observed by  many swedes today but are often drained  of any outright connection to teachings  or imagery from christianity  christmas is a big deal in sweden but to  many the birth of jesus christ isn't  really that relevant or considered  for most the holiday is about gathering  together as a family eating certain  foods  getting presents and even watching  donald duck on television  much more so than it has anything to do  with christ  a term that has been coined to denote  this kind of phenomenon is  post-christian  many swedes are so-called post-christian  in the sense that they have stopped  identifying with the organized religion  of christianity as such but still engage  and participate in a culture that is  infused with christianity take part in  rituals that originate in the religion  or have certain emotional attachments to  these things we love our rituals and  traditions just don't get too religious  about it  all saints day is the same and that is  what i mean when i say that too many  swedes all saints day is really all  souls day because when all saints day is  celebrated few ever think about the  saints and martyrs of christianity  ask any sweden they will tell you that  on all saints day we simply remember  those who have passed  in other words the more overtly  christian aspects of the holiday is  downplayed in favor of a more general  commemoration of the dead on all saints  day families will often gather together  many will visit the graves of loved ones  who have passed perhaps a grandmother or  a father  candles are lit and placed by their  graves and the day is spent trying to  remember and appreciate those loved ones  who have moved on  in stockholm in particular it's quite  common to visit a large cemetery called  shukugorden here as well as in many  other cemeteries most of the graves are  covered with candles creating a kind of  sea of lights in the darkness at least  after the sun has set which is quite a  beautiful sight  even if you don't have a loved one in  this cemetery in particular it has  become a local custom of sorts to visit  on this day to walk around looking at  the lights and the graves  it's a quite solemn atmosphere one where  people will try to give an extra thought  to those who have passed as well as  mortality in general a kind of  collective memento morai  as we saw too many people this has  little to do with christianity but  instead takes the form of a more  universal and secular day of  commemorating the dead even though the  tradition itself clearly does come from  a christian background of course the  church and those active in it will  emphasize it on more clearly christian  grounds and often have services and  vigils during these days in a more quote  unquote traditional way but the varied  ways that this fascinating tradition is  celebrated can really show us how  religions always impact and are impacted  by different local contexts but can also  give us a look into the way that  religion and secularism function and are  in conversation with each other in  contemporary post-christian sweden  i hope those celebrating have a  meaningful all saints and old souls day  and that we are blessed by the memories  of those who are not with us anymore  i'll see you next time  [Music]  you

